Title: TEMPORARY: name the module in delay-load failure crashes
Origin: electron-legacy (this repo)

A delay-load failure on legacy Windows is diagnostically silent. The hook ends
in NOTREACHED(), which in a release build is just base::ImmediateCrash() -- an
int3 -- so Windows reports APPCRASH with exception code 0x80000003 and an
offset, and nothing else. szDll is handed to DEBUG_ALIAS_FOR_CSTR and a crash
key, both of which only pay off if a minidump reaches Crashpad; neither helps
someone sitting in front of a Vista box.

That cost us a whole diagnostic cycle: symbolising the WER offset identified
HandleDelayLoadFailureCommon as the faulting function, which narrowed the
problem to "some delay-loaded import", but the module name was not recoverable
from the dump's stack, and the delay-load table has 48 candidate DLLs.

So say it out loud before crashing. `reason` is the important half: dliFailLoadLib
means the module is absent and needs a stub, while dliFailGetProc means it is
present but missing an export, which is what third_party/winwrap exists to fix.
The two have nothing to do with each other, and the exception code alone cannot
tell them apart.

Recorded first, displayed second. OutputDebugString and a file in %TEMP%
come first because they are kernel32 and cannot fail the way displaying can,
and because a file is the only thing that survives a sandboxed child or a
process that dies before a debugger attaches. A message box follows, since
having to go and find the file is a poor trade when the failure can simply be
put on screen.

The box is deliberately not a plain MessageBoxW call. user32 is itself delay
loaded in this binary, so calling it through the import thunk would have the
hook re-enter the delay load machinery from inside its own failure path -- and
a user32 export is the most likely thing to have failed in the first place.
GetModuleHandleW plus GetProcAddress bypasses the thunk entirely and loads
nothing, so an unmapped user32 means no box rather than a second failure.

Wide throughout; delayimp.h types szDll and szProcName as LPCSTR regardless of
UNICODE, so %hs embeds them without a manual conversion.

DROP THIS PATCH BEFORE SHIPPING. It is a debugging aid for the XP/Vista port,
not behaviour anyone wants in a release: it writes a file and shows a dialog
from a crash path.

diff --git a/chrome/common/win/delay_load_failure_support.cc b/chrome/common/win/delay_load_failure_support.cc
index bd9ef9a666..c0f41fef1c 100644
--- a/chrome/common/win/delay_load_failure_support.cc
+++ b/chrome/common/win/delay_load_failure_support.cc
@@ -6,6 +6,9 @@
 #include <windows.h>
 
 #include <delayimp.h>
+#include <stdio.h>
+
+#include <iterator>
 
 #include "base/check.h"
 #include "base/debug/alias.h"
@@ -25,6 +28,100 @@ FARPROC WINAPI HandleDelayLoadFailureCommon(unsigned reason,
   SCOPED_CRASH_KEY_STRING256("DelayLoad", "ModuleName", dll_name);
   SCOPED_CRASH_KEY_NUMBER("DelayLoad", "LastError", dll_info->dwLastError);
 
+  // TEMPORARY, for the XP/Vista port. In a release build NOTREACHED() below
+  // raises STATUS_BREAKPOINT without emitting anything readable, so WER reports
+  // a bare 0x80000003 and szDll survives only in a minidump. Say it out loud
+  // instead.
+  //
+  // `reason` splits the problem in two, and the halves need opposite fixes:
+  // dliFailLoadLib means the module is absent (a stub DLL is needed), while
+  // dliFailGetProc means it is present but missing an export (winwrap's job).
+  // szProcName is a union with dwOrdinal and is only meaningful for the latter.
+  //
+  // delayimp.h types szDll and szProcName as LPCSTR, so they are narrow
+  // regardless of UNICODE; %hs embeds them in a wide string without a manual
+  // conversion.
+  //
+  // Every branch formats rather than copies: wcscpy_s's template overload
+  // trips -Wunsafe-buffer-usage-in-libc-call, which _snwprintf_s with an
+  // explicit count does not.
+  wchar_t proc[128];
+  if (reason != dliFailGetProc) {
+    _snwprintf_s(proc, std::size(proc), _TRUNCATE, L"%s", L"(n/a)");
+  } else if (dll_info->dlp.fImportByName) {
+    _snwprintf_s(proc, std::size(proc), _TRUNCATE, L"%hs",
+                 dll_info->dlp.szProcName);
+  } else {
+    _snwprintf_s(proc, std::size(proc), _TRUNCATE, L"ordinal #%u",
+                 dll_info->dlp.dwOrdinal);
+  }
+
+  wchar_t message[512];
+  int length = _snwprintf_s(
+      message, std::size(message), _TRUNCATE,
+      L"winwrap delay load failure\r\n"
+      L"reason=%u (3=dliFailLoadLib, 4=dliFailGetProc)\r\n"
+      L"dll=%hs\r\n"
+      L"proc=%s\r\n"
+      L"dwLastError=%lu\r\n",
+      reason, dll_info->szDll, proc, dll_info->dwLastError);
+  if (length < 0) {
+    length = 0;
+  }
+
+  // Recorded before it is displayed, and recorded by means that cannot fail
+  // the way displaying it can. Both of these are kernel32, which is linked
+  // normally.
+  ::OutputDebugStringW(message);
+
+  // And a file, because a process that dies before a debugger is attached
+  // leaves nothing else behind. Truncating rather than appending: the first
+  // failure is fatal, so there is only ever one per run, and a fresh file is
+  // easier to trust than a growing one.
+  // Composed in one call rather than appended in place: indexing past a
+  // returned length is pointer arithmetic the unsafe-buffers plugin rejects,
+  // and GetTempPathW already guarantees the trailing separator.
+  wchar_t directory[MAX_PATH];
+  DWORD directory_length = ::GetTempPathW(MAX_PATH, directory);
+  if (directory_length != 0 && directory_length < MAX_PATH) {
+    wchar_t path[MAX_PATH];
+    _snwprintf_s(path, std::size(path), _TRUNCATE,
+                 L"%swinwrap-delayload.txt", directory);
+    HANDLE file =
+        ::CreateFileW(path, GENERIC_WRITE, FILE_SHARE_READ, nullptr,
+                      CREATE_ALWAYS, FILE_ATTRIBUTE_NORMAL, nullptr);
+    if (file != INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE) {
+      DWORD written = 0;
+      // UTF-16 with a byte order mark, so Notepad on the target reads it.
+      const wchar_t bom = 0xFEFF;
+      ::WriteFile(file, &bom, sizeof(bom), &written, nullptr);
+      ::WriteFile(file, message,
+                  static_cast<DWORD>(length) * sizeof(wchar_t), &written,
+                  nullptr);
+      ::CloseHandle(file);
+    }
+  }
+
+  // Now that the failure is on disk regardless, put it on screen as well,
+  // which saves going to look for the file in the common case.
+  //
+  // Not a plain MessageBoxW call. user32 is itself delay loaded in this
+  // binary, so calling it through the import thunk would have this hook
+  // re-enter the delay load machinery from inside its own failure path -- and
+  // the most likely thing to have failed is a user32 export. Going through an
+  // already-loaded module handle bypasses the thunk completely:
+  // GetModuleHandleW never loads anything, so if user32 is not mapped yet
+  // there is simply no box, and the file above is still there.
+  if (HMODULE user32 = ::GetModuleHandleW(L"user32.dll")) {
+    using MessageBoxWFunction = int(WINAPI*)(HWND, LPCWSTR, LPCWSTR, UINT);
+    auto show = reinterpret_cast<MessageBoxWFunction>(
+        ::GetProcAddress(user32, "MessageBoxW"));
+    if (show) {
+      show(nullptr, message, L"Delay load failure",
+           MB_OK | MB_ICONERROR | MB_SETFOREGROUND | MB_TOPMOST);
+    }
+  }
+
   // Deterministically crash here. Returning 0 from the hook would likely result
   // in the process crashing anyway, but in a form that might trigger undefined
   // behavior or be hard to diagnose. See https://crbug.com/40223662.
